# PART 8 — VALIDATION ENGINE

Create a deterministic validation package independent of the UI.

## 8.1 Validation levels

Level 1: AI response validation.

Validate structure, required data, expected output and configurable response-quality rules.

Level 2: Execution validation.

Validate that the intended tool/API was called with authorised inputs and a traceable operation identity.

Level 3: Business outcome validation.

Query the authoritative business system and check whether the required state actually exists.

## 8.2 Verification contract

Create a common verification interface.

Example:

```ts
interface OutcomeVerifier {
  verify(input: {
    journeyId: string;
    merchantReference: string;
    expectedState: string;
    operationId: string;
  }): Promise<{
    verified: boolean;
    actualState: string;
    sourceSystem: string;
    evidenceReference: string;
    checkedAt: string;
    reason?: string;
  }>;
}
```

Each integration adapter must implement this contract.

## 8.3 Rules

- Agent-reported success is not verified success.
- Missing evidence means unverified.
- A timeout must not automatically mean failure.
- A timeout must not automatically trigger a duplicate side effect.
- Query the source of truth before repeating uncertain operations.
- Use idempotency keys.
- Apply bounded retries and exponential backoff.
- Respect source-system consistency delays.
- Escalate unresolved operations.
- Record all verification decisions.

## 8.4 State machine

Implement:

```text
NOT_STARTED
    ↓
RUNNING
    ↓
REPORTED_SUCCESS
    ↓
VERIFYING
    ├── VERIFIED
    │      ↓
    │   NEXT_STAGE
    │
    ├── MISMATCH
    │      ↓
    │   BLOCKED
    │      ↓
    │   RECHECK / RECOVERY
    │
    ├── SOURCE_UNAVAILABLE
    │      ↓
    │   VERIFICATION_PENDING
    │
    └── NON_RECOVERABLE
           ↓
         NEEDS_HUMAN
```

Other terminal states:

- FAILED
- CANCELLED
- COMPLETED

A journey can only be marked VERIFIED when every required business gate has passed.

